A||N||16||chris6878||How would this v8 push the fiero?||||5||
Z||000000||chris6878||02-24-2000||01:33 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.paceparts.com/fast_burn_385_engine.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.paceparts.com/fast_burn_385_engine.htm</A> <P>How would this V8 perform in the Fiero... <BR>I am planning on doing a v8 conversion this summer to my 85 GT. I am also planning on using the v8 archie basic kit. Would this 350 work with the kit...? Its got a steep price but a nice engine..       <P>What would be an estimated price for a  machine shop too build an engine such as this? Also...  I have read that the Fiero Tranny could hold the hp... and i have read it couldnt. What should one d to prepare the tranny.. (mine is an auto)<P>Batboy-<BR>How is the handling on your V8 fiero?<P>thanks,||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||02-24-2000||04:40 PM||||That engine is similar to the one I have half built for the Batmobile right now except I'm using forged pistons, not hypereutectic pistons (high silicone pistons = hyper). I'll have mine built for about $1000 less using better parts. Of course, I'm shopping around for the best deal on parts and doing all the assembly work myself. A conservative estimate is 400+ ponies using roller cam, lifters, and rockers.<P>The handling of the Batmobile is getting better as I do more work and mods. My wallet is getting lighter too. When I first got it, it handled better than my old big block 68 Chevelle, but I was a little disappointed that it didn't handle nearly as well as my 87 SE that I already had for several years. The front end gets light and "floaty" at triple digits (scary). Lots of torque steer and with all that weight in the back, it's not real predictable when you're driving hard through the curves.<P>Here is my 12 step program to correct this situation on a non-88 Fiero with a V8. 1) Replace all worn out steering and suspension parts. 2) Add better brakes with vented rotors, calipers with bigger pistons, and carbon metallic pads. 3) Take weight off the rear. I'm doing this by moving the battery (about 25 lbs. less weight) from the rear trunk to the front under the spare tire and by using aluminum heads (about 40 lbs. less weight). 4) Add a front air dam. 5) Install a rear sway bar. 5) Install polyurethane control arm and sway bar bushings. 6) Install lower, stiffer front springs. 7) New premium gas shocks and struts. 8) Install rear coil-overs kit and bump steer kit. 9) Replace cradle bushings with poly ones. 10) Adding a torque rod to the engine to suppliment the existing dogbone. 11) Use bigger wheels 12) New performance high speed rated tires.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||02-24-2000||04:46 PM||||I'm sure she'd run like a scallded dog but why spend the money just go buy a LT-1 out of a wrecked camaro/fierbird  you'll have the HP an still have decent gas mileage Billy Graham in b-ham alabama has them with the harness and computer for less than just your motor... (4th Gen F-body scrap yard) On the net not sure of web site address... i know he has one..  Unless you just have to have a carb... then HELL YEAH FB ZZ4 is the way to go...||||reg||1
Z||000003||chris6878||02-24-2000||05:07 PM||||I would much rather have a carb...  to much head aches with the fuel injections...   fuel injection would be better on gas milage though... performance too...  but.. too much hassle for me..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000004||1FST2M6||02-24-2000||06:49 PM||||Theres an old saying (i'm not old enough to say it however i am old enough to to understand it) "evn the worst fuel injection is better than the best carb" or somthing to that effect. After messing with the Carbs on my 79 vette never able to get it running "just right" I bought the Holey Pro-jection unit.. MUCH easier.. the computer tells you what is wrong instead of you having to figure out what you did wrong... went from 4mpg (Holley 900 3 barrel) to about 15 mpg and more HP!!! Obviously its personal preferance and how deep your pockets are but seems really expensive for that FB ZZ4 and you still have to buy all the accessories...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||02-24-2000||08:04 PM||||The only thing I would suggest(other than 3800s are nice)is to keep in mind that emissions testing will be coming to all states soon and they say that this motor won't pass. You could wind up with a real barn burner that you can't use without pouring in a bunch more $$.||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||02-24-2000||08:26 PM||||Good call Phil! all the more reason to get an LT-1. It is already set up to pass emissions.... and it looks just as cool... and besides.. there are not many cars that will be able to touch you with an LT-1... ZZ4 is nice but LT1 or heck.. LT-4... more expensive but passes emissions and is STOOOOOPID strong!!!  OH yeah! You'd be hard pressed to get a carb motor to pass emissions.. with out one of those lame looking smog pumps that were crammed in the 3rd gen Camaro and mustangs... Lame but effective||||reg||1
Z||000007||pherder||02-24-2000||08:50 PM||||The problem with the LT-1 is it has reverse coolant flow and requires that cam shaft driven water pump.  It sticks out so far that Archie has to supply different axles so that the engine will fit.  (I.e. he ends up moving the engine assembly over 2" to the left like another V8 swapper does with all his V8 swaps.)<P>Paul||||reg||8
Z||000008||chris6878||02-24-2000||08:56 PM||||How much horse power are these LT 1's . What about using an electric water pump.. or is the regular type a better selection...  the electric pump will give you more room in the wheel well area wouldn't it?||||reg||3
Z||000009||Phil||02-24-2000||09:45 PM||||Hate to throw cold water on the electric water pump issue, but I don't believe that there is an elec. pump that would pump enough water to cool the engine under sustained driving conditions.||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FST2M6||02-24-2000||10:41 PM||||yeah I don't know of a manufacturer of a daily use electric water pump.. I have the  Summit Catalog that arrived in the mail yesterday and they all say "Not for daily use..." or "Not Streetable"  crappy! LT-1 belts out between 300-340 hp...  thats what the Factory specs say in the back of Road & Track  (Camaro / Vette)... oh well ..  OK then on to Plan B. the old Tune Port Injection 350 out of a 86-88 vette!  285ish HP 315 Lbft of torque... that should do ok.. and still pass emissions!<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 02-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||02-24-2000||11:40 PM||||Guys, this is my playtoy and my greatest love about hotrodding is building my own high performance engine. It makes victory so much sweeter! The aluminum heads are what breaks my budget on this project. I haven't got 'em yet, but I have to budget a grand for new ones. I might scour the next swap meet for a good cheap used set.<P>This is what I got and why I'm building it myself. A 1988 Z-28 "seasoned" roller 4 bolt main block, magnafluxed, bored out 0.030" and then decked a few thousands for straightness. Heavy duty Chevy truck rods completely reconditioned. Crankshaft (one piece rear seal) was magnafluxed, checked for straightness, then turned 0.010". A Z-28 high performance oil pump, TRW forged pistons, Perfect Circle rings, Fel-Pro gaskets, and Clevite 77 bearings were used. Comp Cams Xtreme 270 roller cam, roller lifters, roller rocker arms, topped with a Edelbrock Performer RPM intake manifold and Performer carb. Haven't got the heads yet, but they'll be 2.02" intakes, 1.6" exhaust valves and about 9.5:1 compression with flat top pistons. I haven't got these yet either: Hooker block hugger street rod headers, custom dual exhaust with Flowmaster mufflers, complete Accel ignition system, etc.<P>I know a computer program is not the real world and only the dyno will tell for sure afterwards, but my Desktop Dyno says 413 hp @5,500 RPM and 438 tq @ 4,000-4,500 RPM. I've plugged in the combinations for actual dynoed engines and I've found the program to be very accurate. Maybe up to 10 hp more generous than real gross horsepower. Still should be in the ballpark of 400 ponies, but the best part is it produces over 400 ft/lbs. of torque from 2,500 RPM through well over 5,000 RPM, WOW! Bad part is that if you want these kind of numbers, ya gotta pay da price. I use a factory water pump mounted upside down with the pulley running outside the engine compartment just inside the right rear tire.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/batpulley.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||02-24-2000||11:51 PM||||Just wish I had the money for somthing like that... and i though my car was quick.... Nothing sound sweeter than a small block all  decked out... ((( maybe one day ))) Till that day i'll just drool... Any V8 Fieros in the Atlanta area? need a passenger for a few minutes? ||||reg||1
